\f1\fs22 Midler wondered how many of Shakti\'92s mercenaries had flipped sides.\
On the ultimate viewing, he paused on the package where Alexander was linking demonic possession with three decrepit old men singing a silly ditty and, quite frankly, making pork chops of themselves, while Eloise Shakti apparently presided over the whole unholy mess. Eloise 
\f2\i and
\f1\i0  Sarah Jacobson, Midler noted, were on that platform\'97a fact the show drew no attention to. Clearly Alexander did not want to implicate his trophy wife in all the spell-casting. Another fact the broadcast side-stepped was the number of Elvi in the crowd\'97easily half by Midler\'92s estimates on the footage he\'92d seen.\
The younger Shakti had told him everything would have changed after the show. If he thought it was time to seize power and air his family grievances on live-broadcast TV, Midler could work with that. If he wanted to feed his dear mater to sharks, then okay, there were worse ways to die.\
Or maybe not. No matter. Midler had seen blood in the water before.\
Midler had not expected a sizeable contingent of Shakti security to be baying for Eloise to be fed to sharks, and none to arrive to save her. Instead, Eloise was rescued by her heavily pregnant daughter-in-law, probably to whisk her away to a safe exile.\
Whatever this was, it was no longer a military operation, which supported his view it never was one.\
The Elvi unsettled him. While Midler reviewed the broadcast, there was an Elvi band playing in the bar downstairs, and potentially thousands more roaming the village. Midler reassessed the tactical value of them clogging the streets and distracting the locals, and weighed them less as a buffer on his flanks and more of a mildly entertaining horde of Visigoths on his doorstep.\
Maybe they were nothing. Alexander\'92s portrayal of mass mind-control was just a tacky music festival, though why Eloise Shakti wanted to attend was beyond his reckoning. Maybe the Elvi just got into everything these days, like possums in your roof.\
Major Midler hated fighting possums.\
What remained were his orders: neutralise Shakti and take the compound. Currently, he knew the status of neither mother nor son. With the right timing, there might remain nothing for Midler to do but drive in and claim the compound. \
Midler reviewed the aerial view of Sublime Point. The auditorium was dark; the rest of the compound was not. Lamps still lit the internal roads, and the perimeter weapons had neither exploded nor thoughtfully turned themselves towards Eloise\'92s house. And there appeared to be an outdoor concert on the carpark.\
Nothing required him to find out today which way they would shoot.\
An afghan hound came and licked his hand. It had been washed, blow-dried and groomed within a puppy\'92s tail  of its canine identity. Midler looked out window to Christmas carollers on the mall, and crumpled in his hand the terse letter on impeccable stock from the golf club committee that he vacate the function room and quit the golf course. He had literally been consigned to the doghouse by both military order and an entitled social committee.\
There was but one course of action remaining. He called for his adjutant, and retrieved his cigar from the rim of a saucer bearing the golf club\'92s crest, then found some dog biscuits and let the afghan eat them off the chinaware.\
His adjutant appeared at the door. \'93Sir.\'94\
\'93Suspend Operation Herm\'e8s.\'94
